Z||000005||batboy||03-01-2000||07:19 AM||||That pic is my Batmobile after the front end work was done, but nothing was done to the back suspension yet. I used S/T sport lowering springs and slightly smaller tires to drop the nose about 2 inches. I have a rear coil-over conversion kit ordered, so I hope to drop the rear about 1 inch, then add new tires and wheels. By the way, the car is parked on a slight slope which makes it look swoopier and more jacked up too. Actually with the rear end up in the air, handling will not be as good.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||03-01-2000||08:24 AM||||There is very little downforce on the front of a Fiero at 100+mph?! Due to the design of the radiator cavity, the front end actually gets lighter as speeds increase. At 100mph, there is 120lbs of lift on the nose.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||03-01-2000||09:34 AM||||The back tires are also two sizes bigger than the front in that pic.<P>Fiero56, I've always complained of that light feeling in the nose of my V8 Fiero. I'm hoping hood louvers and a front air dam will help. I'm also moving the battery up to the front too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||03-01-2000||08:28 PM||||I wanted a Ferrari 512BB when I was a teenager,but,if you really want the car to look like a postal truck,then get some springs from a larger GM vehicle with the same design of spring(Buick,Olds).Maybe some Yokohama A509s(very sticky tires)to help the violent oversteer that will come with this new look too,would be thoughtful.||||reg||1
